# Plan: replace the offsetWidth reflow trick with Web Animations in src/ui/audioMeters.ts

All line numbers below are for `src/ui/audioMeters.ts` at 8a1fecb. Line numbers drift as you edit, so search by name.

## What I read (verified)

- `blink(el, color)` (~L743) sets `transition: none`, writes the jump colour, does `void el.offsetWidth`, then sets `transition = FADE`. `FADE = "background-color 0.3s ease-out"` (L418) is also already in the fill's own `cssText` (~L425). That means the fill carries the transition permanently, and `blink` re-arming it is only needed because it turned it off.
- `flashRow(el)` (~L759) does the same with `boxShadow`. It then uses `requestAnimationFrame` to clear the inline shadow and `setTimeout(…, ROW_FLASH_MS)` (900) to clear the inline `transition`. `ROW_FLASH_MS` is used only there. Its only caller is `revealRow` (~L2862).
- `createMeterRow` (~L803): `flash(color = "#fff")` calls `blink(fill, color)` and sets `flashed = true`.
  - `setValue` writes `fill.style.backgroundColor = restColor` when `flashed` is set, then clears it.
  - `setFillColor` skips the inline write while `flashed` is set.
  - `restColor` starts as `spec.accent`.
  - `flash` has two callers that I found: `section.flash(HOT_RED)` (~L2824). `blink` is also called from the meter row's `flash` only. I did not grep for other `.flash(` callers beyond the output of `grep "flash("`, which showed only that one.
- `createTempoBlock` (~L920):
  - `restColor` starts as `withAlpha(BEAT_COLOR, 0.25)` and is re-derived from `lock` only when the quantised `step` changes.
  - `settle()` writes the resting bg, shadow and transform inline.
  - `update(lock, beat)` calls `settle()` on the frame after a beat (via the `lit` flag). On a beat it sets `transition: none`, writes the lit look (`#fff`, `0 0 6px 1px ${BEAT_COLOR}`, `scale(1.6)`), does `void dot.offsetWidth`, then sets `transition = DOT_EASE`.
  - `tempoDotStyle` (~L464) has `transition: ${DOT_EASE}`. `DOT_EASE` is `"background-color 0.55s ease-out, box-shadow 0.55s ease-out, transform 0.55s ease-out"` (L462).
  - The single call site is `tempo.update(anim?.tempoLock ?? 0, !!anim?.metronomeBeat)` (~L2795).
- `vite.config.ts` `target: "es2017"` only lowers syntax. The comment says it exists for webOS 5/6 and Tizen TVs. `tests/es2017Apis.test.ts` lists the files that run on a TV, and `audioMeters.ts` is NOT in that list, because it is the controls panel. So there is no API ban to satisfy for this file.
- `src/ui/powerCard.ts` ~L670–700 already uses `el.animate(...)` with a `typeof el.animate !== "function"` guard. Follow that style.
- `vitest.config.ts` runs `environment: "node"` and no DOM library is in `package.json`. Tests that need DOM stub globals with `vi.stubGlobal`.
- `audioMeters.ts` has no header-comment line about reflow. The reflow reasoning lives in the doc comments of `blink`, `flashRow` and the inline comments, and in the `update()` doc. There are no docs outside this file to update (grep for reflow/offsetWidth/jump-then-fade in docs found only unrelated scene records).

UNVERIFIED:
- Whether importing `audioMeters.ts` in a node vitest run works. It imports `deviceMenu.ts` and `controlsTheme.ts`, and I did not check for top-level `document`/`window` use. That is why the plan puts the testable logic in a new DOM-free module and does NOT ask for a test that imports `audioMeters.ts`.
- Which browser versions support implicit keyframes. The plan avoids them: always give explicit start and end keyframes.
- Whether `docs/status.md` wants a dated line. Run `/ship` and `/wrap`, which handle that.

## Design

The animation, not inline styles, now carries the lit look. Inline style stays at the resting value the whole time, so there is no write-then-read and no forced style/layout. A new beat cancels the previous animation. Without `fill`, the animation stops affecting the element when it ends, leaving the inline resting look.

### 1. New file `src/ui/flashFade.ts` (DOM-free, no imports)

Start it with a header comment, per CLAUDE.md. Do not copy numbers from the code into comments. The header should say:
- what a flash is: a one-frame event shown as a jump to a lit look that fades to the resting look;
- why it uses `Element.animate` rather than a transition: the old trick of switching the transition off, writing, reading `offsetWidth` and switching it on forced a style-and-layout pass in the middle of the frame, once per beat;
- that the animation sits above inline style, so the caller keeps the resting look inline;
- that it does nothing where `animate` is missing (older browsers), so the flash is skipped and nothing else breaks;
- that the keyframe builders are separate pure functions so they can be tested without a DOM.

Exports:

```ts
/** The part of Element.animate this file uses; HTMLElement satisfies it. */
export interface Flashable {
  animate?: (keyframes: Keyframe[], options: KeyframeAnimationOptions) => { cancel(): void };
}
export interface FlashTiming { duration: number; easing: string }

export interface Flash {
  /** Cancels the previous flash, then plays this one. Returns false if the element cannot animate. */
  play(keyframes: Keyframe[]): boolean;
}

export function createFlash(el: Flashable, timing: FlashTiming): Flash {
  let running: { cancel(): void } | null = null;
  return {
    play(keyframes) {
      running?.cancel();
      running = null;
      if (typeof el.animate !== "function") return false;
      running = el.animate(keyframes, timing);
      return true;
    },
  };
}

/** Fill flash: jump colour, then the resting colour. */
export function fillFlashFrames(lit: string, rest: string): Keyframe[] {
  return [{ backgroundColor: lit }, { backgroundColor: rest }];
}

/** Beat dot: white in a halo and enlarged, back to the resting tint, no halo, normal size. */
export function dotFlashFrames(glow: string, rest: string): Keyframe[] {
  return [
    { backgroundColor: "#fff", boxShadow: `0 0 6px 1px ${glow}`, transform: "scale(1.6)" },
    { backgroundColor: rest, boxShadow: "0 0 0 0 transparent", transform: "scale(1)" },
  ];
}

/** Row ring: the white ring and glow, then nothing. */
export function rowFlashFrames(): Keyframe[] {
  return [
    { boxShadow: "0 0 0 1px #fff, 0 0 16px 2px rgba(255,255,255,0.5)" },
    { boxShadow: "0 0 0 0 transparent" },
  ];
}
```

Pitfalls:
- `Keyframe` and `KeyframeAnimationOptions` are DOM lib types, available because tsconfig `lib` includes DOM. Use `import type`-free usage, since they are globals.
- `HTMLElement` is assignable to `Flashable` because `Animation` has `cancel()`. If `tsc` complains about parameter types, widen the `keyframes` parameter to `Keyframe[] | PropertyIndexedKeyframes | null`.
- Keep the numbers (halo size, scale, ring) copied from the old code exactly. The look must not change.

### 2. Edits in `src/ui/audioMeters.ts`

Add `import { createFlash, dotFlashFrames, fillFlashFrames, rowFlashFrames, type Flash } from "./flashFade.ts";` with the other relative imports.

**Constants**
- Replace `FADE` (L418) with a duration constant plus a string built from it. Keep `FADE` because the fill's `cssText` still uses it for `setFillColor`/`setValue` colour changes:
  ```ts
  const FILL_FADE_MS = 300;
  const FILL_EASE = "ease-out";
  const FADE = `background-color ${FILL_FADE_MS}ms ${FILL_EASE}`;
  ```
- Replace `DOT_EASE` (L462) with `const DOT_FADE: FlashTiming = { duration: 550, easing: "ease-out" }` (import the type). Remove `transition: ${DOT_EASE};` from `tempoDotStyle`. The dot's only changes are the flash, and the old transition would now fight the animation whenever `settle()` rewrites a changed rest tint.
- Replace `ROW_FLASH_MS` (900) with `const ROW_FADE: FlashTiming = { duration: 600, easing: "ease-out" }`. The old 900 was just the cleanup timer for the inline transition; 600 is the fade itself.

**`blink` (~L743)** takes the resting colour and a per-element `Flash`:
```ts
/** Flash a fill to `color`, then fade to `rest` (flashFade.ts). */
function blink(flash: Flash, color: string, rest: string): void {
  flash.play(fillFlashFrames(color, rest));
}
```
Rewrite the doc comment: it no longer jumps the inline style or re-arms a transition.

**`createMeterRow`**
- After `fill` is created add `const fillFlash = createFlash(fill, { duration: FILL_FADE_MS, easing: FILL_EASE });`.
- `flash(color = "#fff")` becomes `blink(fillFlash, color, restColor);`. Remove `flashed = true`.
- Remove the `flashed` variable, the `if (flashed) { fill.style.backgroundColor = restColor; flashed = false; }` block in `setValue`, and the `!flashed` guard in `setFillColor` (it now writes inline unconditionally). Inline style is never left on the lit colour now, so the guard has nothing to protect.
- Update the `flash` doc comment ("fades back on the next setValue()" is no longer true): the fill plays a flash from `color` to the resting colour and the inline colour is untouched.
- Pitfall: the end keyframe is `restColor` at flash time. If `setFillColor` changes it mid-flash, the animation ends on the old colour and the inline colour has already moved. Accept this (the fade is 300 ms).

**`flashRow` (~L759)**
```ts
const rowFlashes = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, Flash>();
function flashRow(el: HTMLElement): void {
  let flash = rowFlashes.get(el);
  if (!flash) rowFlashes.set(el, (flash = createFlash(el, ROW_FADE)));
  flash.play(rowFlashFrames());
}
```
Delete the `requestAnimationFrame` and `setTimeout` bodies and their comment. Rewrite the doc comment to say it flashes the row's own ring without touching inline style.
- Known small change: the old code faded to the row's hover ring when the pointer was over it. The new end keyframe is a transparent shadow, so a hovered row's ring reappears when the flash ends. Check by eye. If it is visibly worse, switch to a single keyframe (implicit end) for the row only. Implicit keyframes are UNVERIFIED for old browsers, but this panel is not a TV page.

**`createTempoBlock`**
- Remove `let lit = false;`.
- After `dot` is created add `const dotFlash = createFlash(dot, DOT_FADE);`.
- In `update()` drop the `if (lit) { settle(); lit = false; }` block. Replace the `if (beat) { … }` body with:
  ```ts
  if (beat) {
    settle(); // inline stays at the resting look; the animation draws the flash above it
    dotFlash.play(dotFlashFrames(BEAT_COLOR, restColor));
  }
  ```
  `settle()` is still used at ~L945 (initial write). Keep it.
- Rewrite the `update()` doc comment ("a lit dot eases back to it on the frame after its beat"): on a beat the dot flashes from the lit look to the current resting tint, and a new beat cancels the flash in progress.
- Behavior to keep: the rest tint is rewritten inline only on a beat (as before, `lock` changes alone do not repaint the dot). Do not add a write in the step-change branch.
- Also check the ~L432–435 block comment above `BEAT_COLOR`. It still reads correctly (white-to-orange). Do not change its wording unless it names the transition.

### 3. Tests: new `tests/flashFade.test.ts` (node env, no DOM)

Use a fake element: `{ animate: vi.fn((kf, opt) => ({ cancel: vi.fn() })) }`. Collect the returned handles so you can assert on them.

1. **Cancel on re-trigger.** `createFlash(fake, {duration: 550, easing: "ease-out"})`. Call `play(A)`, `play(B)`, `play(C)`. Expect `animate` called 3 times with A, B, C and the timing object `{duration: 550, easing: "ease-out"}`. The first handle's `cancel` was called once, the second's once, the third's zero times. This fails if `play` stops cancelling, cancels twice, or cancels the new animation.
2. **No `animate` available.** `createFlash({}, timing).play(x)` returns `false` and does not throw; calling `play` twice stays safe. It fails if the `typeof` guard is removed.
3. **Dot keyframes.** `dotFlashFrames("#ff3b30", "#ff3b3099")` equals exactly two frames: first `{ backgroundColor: "#fff", boxShadow: "0 0 6px 1px #ff3b30", transform: "scale(1.6)" }`, second `{ backgroundColor: "#ff3b3099", boxShadow: "0 0 0 0 transparent", transform: "scale(1)" }`. The test fails if the glow colour, the rest colour or the order is wrong. Use values that differ from each other (not `#fff` for both).
4. **Fill and row keyframes.** `fillFlashFrames("#00ff88", "#123456")` is `[{backgroundColor:"#00ff88"},{backgroundColor:"#123456"}]`. A swap of lit and rest fails it. `rowFlashFrames()` has a first frame whose `boxShadow` contains both `"#fff"` and `"16px"`, and a last frame of `"0 0 0 0 transparent"`.
5. **Real rest tint through the existing helper.** In the same file, `import { withAlpha } from "../src/ui/controlsTheme.ts"` ONLY if that import works in node (it has a `?url` font import; the vitest config comment says that is handled). If it errors, skip this case. Case: `withAlpha("#ff3b30", 0.25 + 0.6 * (10 / 20))` is `"#ff3b308c"` (0.55 * 255 = 140.25, which rounds to 140 = 0x8c), and `dotFlashFrames` with that rest ends on that string.

Do NOT write tests that import `audioMeters.ts` (UNVERIFIED that it loads in node). The wiring is covered by typecheck and the manual checks below.

## Verification commands (run in this order)

1. `npm run typecheck` (`tsc -b && tsc -b server`). Watch for `noUnusedLocals`: `FADE`, `settle`, `ROW_FLASH_MS` and `DOT_EASE` must either be used or removed.
2. `npx vitest run tests/flashFade.test.ts`, then `npm test`.
3. `grep -n "offsetWidth" src/ui/audioMeters.ts` must return nothing.
4. `npm run build` (confirms the es2017 build still bundles).
5. Manual: `npm run dev`, open `?audio=synthetic&bpm=124`, open the panel, and check by eye:
   - the BPM dot jumps to white with a halo and scale, then fades back, on each beat;
   - the Section bar flashes red on a drop and fades;
   - `revealRow` (via a jack or hint link that jumps to a row) rings the row and fades it.
6. Perf trace: `node docs/scenes/physarum2/scripts/perf/paneltrace.mjs --port <dev port> --card Tempo --seconds 5`. It writes `trace-<port>.json`. Search the trace for `Blink.ForcedStyleAndLayout` events whose stack contains `update` from `audioMeters`. Compare before (8a1fecb) and after; the after run should have none from the beat dot. (UNVERIFIED that the script runs unchanged; it needs Playwright and the dev server's port.)
7. Finish with `/ship`, then `/wrap`. Neither `docs/scenes` nor `docs/status.md` need a scene record, since this is panel code and not a scene.

## Pitfalls summary

- Never write the lit look to inline style; the animation must carry it, or the next resting write will fight it.
- Always `cancel()` the previous animation; without that, rapid beats stack animations and the dot could stay lit.
